<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Kilimanjaro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kilimanjaro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Kilimanjaro is a dormant volcano in north-eastern Tanzania near the Kenyan border and, at 5,895 metres, the highest mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Its summit, Uhuru Peak on the Kibo cone, still carries glaciers that are rapidly shrinking. Kilimanjaro National Park was inscribed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List in 1987, and the mountain attracts tens of thousands of climbers each year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-kilimanjaro.jpg" alt="Mount Kilimanjaro" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Pesterev · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kilimanjaro_from_Amboseli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kilimanjaro is a dormant stratovolcano made up of three volcanic cones: Kibo, the highest, Mawenzi at 5,149 metres and Shira, the lowest at 4,005 metres. Mawenzi and Shira are extinct, while Kibo is dormant and could erupt again. It is the highest free-standing mountain above sea level in the world and one of several mountains arising from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">East African Rift</a>, formed by volcanic activity over two million years ago. Eruptive activity at Shira began about 2.4 million years ago, and Mawenzi and Kibo began erupting about 1 million years ago.</p><p>Its slopes carry montane forests and cloud forests, and several species are endemic to the mountain. Kibo bears a large ice cap and the largest glaciers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and the ice cap has lost over 80% of its extent in the 20th century. Geological evidence shows five successive glacial episodes in the Quaternary period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Chagga people traditionally lived on the southern and eastern slopes in sovereign Chagga states, known as the Chagga Kingdoms until their abolition in 1963 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>. The Chagga have no name for the mountain itself, but call its two peaks Kipoo and Kimawenze, usually rendered Kibo and Mawenzi, meaning spotted and broken top. The origin of the name Kilimanjaro is disputed. It may come from the Chagga calling the mountain unclimbable, which explorers misunderstood as its name. A Chagga tale tells how Tone, who provoked the god Ruwa, was pursued by fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> that threw up hills, including Mawenzi and Kibo.</p><p>The mountain may have been known to non-Africans since antiquity, since Ptolemy records sailors' reports of a moon mountain and a spring lake of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, though these could refer to other East African mountains. The German missionary Johannes Rebmann is credited as the first European to report its existence, in 1848, when he took the white summit for snow, which his guide called cold. After several attempts by Europeans, Hans Meyer reached the highest summit in 1889. Two German climbers made the first ascent of Mawenzi's highest summit on 29 July 1912, and the mountain gave its name to the 1914 Battle of Kilimanjaro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kilimanjaro lies in Tanzania and has been part of Kilimanjaro National Park since 1973. Uhuru Peak, on the rim of Kibo's crater, is its highest point, at 5,895 metres by the figure of a British survey of 1952 that Tanzanian authorities still list. It is one of the Seven Summits and a major hiking destination, with seven established routes to Uhuru Peak. Although not technically challenging, its height carries a serious risk of altitude sickness.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Kilimanjaro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Peters, named Reichskommissar for the <b>Kilimanjaro</b> Region in 1891 under Soden, was recalled in 1892 and in 1897, after three investigations, deprived of his commission, his title and his pension; Kaiser Wilhelm II nevertheless gave him the title of 'retired Reichskommissar' in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Since liberalisation in the mid-1980s the economy has grown steadily, led by mining, tourism around <b>Mount Kilimanjaro</b>, the Serengeti and Zanzibar, and the port of Dar es Salaam, the largest city and an outlet for several landlocked neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
